[[File:Kibitsu Jinja -02.jpg|thumb|220px|right|{{center|At the entrance{{ill|lt=Shimonawa|Q1887421|qid=Q1887421|1=Shimenawa}}}}]]``Kibitsu Shrine'' is located in {{ill|lt=Okayama Prefecture|Q132936|qid=Q132936|1=Okayama Prefecture}}{{ill|lt=Okayama City|Q200078|qid=Q200078|1=Okayama}}{{ill|lt=Kita Ward (Okayama City)|Q855478|qid=Q855478|lt=Kita Ward|1=Kita-ku, Okayama}} {{ill|lt=Shrine|Q845945|qid=Q845945|1=Shinto shrine}} located in {{ill|lt=Kibitsu|Q11412649|qid=Q11412649|1=Kibitsu}}. {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinai Shrine|1=Shikinaisha|qid=Q134917286|lt=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|lt=Meishin Taisha|Q9610964|qid=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}}), {{ill|lt=Bichū|Q1047514|qid=Q1047514|1=Bitchū Province}}{{ill|lt=Ichinomiya|Q1656379|qid=Q1656379|1=Ichinomiya}}. {{ill|lt=Modern shrine rank system|Q712534|qid=Q712534|lt=Old shrine rank|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}} is {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kanpei Chusha|1=UNKNOWN}}, and currently it is {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|qid=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} of {{ill|lt=Shrine Honcho|Q908077|qid=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.

In ``{{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|qid=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}}'' it is listed as ``Kibitsuhiko Shrine.'' {{ill|lt=2025|Q49616|qid=Q49616|1=2025}} ({{ill|lt=Reiwa|Q62609017|qid=Q62609017|1=Reiwa era}}7) {{jalink|current|Q193168} }'s formal name is 'Kibitsu Shrine''' <ref>Currently, Bicchu and Bingo Ichinomiya call themselves ``Kibitsu Shrine,'' but only Bizen Ichinomiya calls itself ``Kibitsuhiko Shrine.'' </ref>.

=== Overview ===
It faces north at the northwest foot of {{ill|lt=Kibi no Nakayama|Q135646238|qid=Q135646238|1=Mount Kibi no Nakayama}} (175 meters above sea level) on the border between {{ill|lt=Bizen Province|Q1047514|qid=Q1047514|1=Bitchū Province}} in the western part of Okayama City. Nakayama in Kibi has been known as {{ill|lt=Shintaiyama|Q1595289|qid=Q1595289|1=Sacred mountains}} since ancient times, and Bizen Province Ichinomiya, {{ill|lt=Kibitsuhiko Shrine|Q500763|qid=Q500763|1=Kibitsuhiko Shrine}}, is enshrined at the northeastern foot of the mountain. Both our company and Kibitsuhiko Shrine enshrine {{ill|lt=Kibitsuhiko no Mikoto|Q65318|qid=Q65318|lt=Okibitsuhiko no Mikoto|1=Kibitsuhiko-no-mikoto}}, who is said to have ruled the area, as the main deity, and the clan of life is enshrined here.

Originally, it was the general guardian of {{ill|lt=Kibikuni|Q1155128|qid=Q1155128|1=Kingdom of Kibi}}, but due to the division of Kibikuni into three kingdoms, it became the Ichinomiya of Bichoku, and its branch spirit is the Ichinomiya of Bizen and {{ill|lt=Bingokuni|Q1049113|qid=Q1049113|1=Bingo Province}} (Bizen: Kibitsuhiko Shrine, Bingo: {{ill|lt=Kibitsu Shrine) (Fukuyama City)|Q6404042|qid=Q6404042|lt=Kibitsu Shrine|1=Kibitsu Shrine (Bingo)}}). For this reason, Kibitsuhiko Shrine in Bizen takes the names of ``Kibisou Chinju'' and ``Sanbi Ichinomiya''. </ref>.The main shrine, which is said to have been built {{ill|lt=Yoshimitsu Ashikaga|Q248381|qid=Q248381|1=Ashikaga Yoshimitsu}}, is the only {{ill|lt=Hiyoku-irimoya-zukuri|Q3196321|qid=Q3196321|1=Kibitsu-zukuri}} (Kibitsu-zukuri) in Japan, and is designated as a {{ill|lt=national treasure|Q1139795|qid=Q1139795|1=National Treasure (Japan)}} along with the worship hall. Additionally, three shrine buildings have been designated as national {{ill|lt=Important Cultural Properties|Q1188622|qid=Q1188622|1=Important Cultural Property (Japan)}}. It is also famous for {{ill|lt=Narukama Shinto ritual|Q11675410|qid=Q11675410|1=narukama shinji}}, which predicts good or bad fortune by the sound of the kettle.<ref name="map" />

It is said that the local politician {{ill|lt=Tsuyoshi Inukai|Q354687|qid=Q354687|1=Inukai Tsuyoshi}} revered Kibitsu Shrine, believing that Inukai Ken-no-Mikoto, the distant ancestor of the Inukai family, was the deity of Okibitsuhiko-no-Mikoto.<ref name="map">{{Cite web|Japanese book|url=https://okayama-kanko.net/sightseeing/_cms/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/85ba4c00217acc9f333e2c349 6cdce52.pdf|title=Kibiji Tourist Map|publisher=Okayama Tourism and Convention Association|date=|accessdate=2025-01-13|pages=9-10}}</ref>. A bronze statue of Tsuyoshi Inukai was erected on the shore of Kamiike Pond, and the company name of Kibitsu Shrine was also written by Tsuyoshi Inukai.The Spring Festival is held on the second Sunday in May, and the Autumn Festival is held on the second Sunday in October. Among these, the Seventy-Five Zentai Ceremony, in which 75 tables are carried from the Gotoden through the corridor to the main shrine, is one of the three major festivals in Okayama Prefecture. 75 Zentai Shinto ritual|publisher=Okayama Prefecture Tourism Federation|date=|accessdate=2025-11-15|pages=}}</ref>.

"{{ill|lt=Plum|Q157763|qid=Q157763|1=Prunus mume}}", "{{ill|lt=Camellia|Q160121|qid=Q160121|1=Camellia japonica}}", "{{ill|lt=Sakura|Q871991|qid=Q871991|1=Cherry blossom}}", "{{ill|lt=Button (Plant)|Q163018|qid=Q163018|lt=Button|1=Paeonia × suffruticosa}}'', ``{{ill|lt=Azalea|Q189393|qid=Q189393|1=Rhododendron}}'', ``{{ill|lt=Satsuki|Q5366359|qid=Q5366359|1=Rhododendron indicum}}'', ``{{ill|lt=Hana iris|Q21558856|qid=Q21558856|1=Iris ensata var. ensata}}'', and ``{{ill|lt=Hydrangea|Q159219|qid=Q159219|1=Hydrangea macrophylla}}'', which are also famous flower spots<ref name="map" />. It is also known as a famous spot for {{ill|lt=autumn leaves|Q1156320|qid=Q1156320|1=Autumn leaf color}} in {{ill|lt=Ginkgo|Q43284|qid=Q43284|1=Ginkgo biloba}}.

=== Enshrined God ===
The following nine deities are enshrined.
; Main enshrined deity
:* {{ill|lt=Kibitsuhiko no Mikoto|Q65318|qid=Q65318|lt=Okibitsuhiko no Mikoto|1=Kibitsuhiko-no-mikoto}} (Ookibitsuhiko no Mikoto)
:*: He was the third {{ill|lt=prince|Q12886381|qid=Q12886381|1=Imperial Prince}} of the 7th {{ill|lt=Emperor Korei|Q313041|qid=Q313041|1=Emperor Kōrei}}, and his original name was ``Hiko Isaserihiko no Mikoto (Isosa Serihiko no Mikoto). {{ill|lt=Emperor Sujin|Q333530|qid=Q333530|1=Emperor Sujin}} In 10 years, he was dispatched to Sanyo Province as one of the Shoguns of the Four Paths, and subdued Kibi with his younger brother Wakahiko Takekibitsuhiko no Mikoto. It is said that his descendants became {{ill|lt=Kunizo|Q2483673|qid=Q2483673|1=Kuni no miyatsuko}} of Kibi, and became {{ill|lt=Kibi clan|Q11412645|qid=Q11412645|lt=Kibi vassal|1=Kibi clan}} of {{ill|lt=ancient|Q41493|qid=Q41493|1=Ancient history}}{{ill|lt=gozoku|Q84066|qid=Q84066|1=Gōzoku}}.
; Aidenjin
:* Momota Yumiya Hime no Mikoto - After Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto.
:* {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Mitomobetsu no Mikoto|1=UNKNOWN}} (Mitomowake no Mikoto) - [[wikt:descendant|descendant]] of Okibitsuhiko no Mikoto.
:* Nakahiko no Mikoto - A descendant of Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto.
:* {{jalink|Chichihayahime no Mikoto|Q10904825|Chichihayahime no Mikoto} - Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto's {{ill|lt=sister|Q10082670|qid=Q10082670|1=elder sister}}.
:* {{ill|lt=Waterikatsuhi Hyakusouhime no Mikoto|Q11387098|qid=Q11387098|1=Yamato-totohi-momoso-hime}} (Yamatoto and Himomosohime no Mikoto) - Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto's older sister.:* Hiko Sasukatawake no Mikoto - {{ill|lt=older brother|Q3972282|qid=Q3972282|1=elder brother}} of Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto.
:* {{ill|lt=Wakayahime no Mikoto|Q11387099|qid=Q11387099|lt=Watakuyahime no Mikoto|1=Princess Yamatototowakayahime}} (Yamatoto and Hiwakayahime no Mikoto) - Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto's {{ill|lt=sister|Q10943095|qid=Q10943095|1=younger sister}}.
:* {{ill|lt=Hikosashima-no-Mikoto|Q11487957|qid=Q11487957|lt=Hikosama-no-Mikoto|1=Hikosashima no Mikoto}} (Hikosamema no Mikoto) - {{ill|lt=younger brother|Q10291472|qid=Q10291472|1=younger brother}} of Daikibitsuhiko-no-Mikoto.
:* {{ill|lt=Wakahiko Mikoto|Q11596350|qid=Q11596350|lt=Wakahiko Takekibitsuhiko no Mikoto|1=Wakatakehiko}} (Wakahiko Takekibitsuhiko no Mikoto) - The younger brother of Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto.

In ancient times, it was known as ``Kibitsu Gosho Daimyojin,'' which consisted of five shrines, the main shrine and four other shrines.

