=== History ===

=== Founding ===
[[File:Kibi's Nakayama (from Kibitsu Shrine).JPG|thumb|220px|right|{{center|Enshrined{{ill|lt=Kibi's Nakayama|Q135646238|qid=Q135646238|1=Mount Kibi no Nakayama}}}}{{small|On the far right is the pine trees on the approach. }}]]
According to the shrine's legend, the enshrined deity, Daikibitsuhiko-no-Mikoto, lived in a thatched shrine at the foot of {{ill|lt=Kibi Nakayama|Q135646238|qid=Q135646238|1=Mount Kibi no Nakayama}}, and was buried at the top of the mountain at the age of 281. Regarding the founding of Kibitsu Shrine, there is a theory that it was founded by {{ill|lt=Emperor Nintoku|Q313119|qid=Q313119|1=Emperor Nintoku}} to honor the achievements of Dai Kibitsuhiko no Mikoto, while a shrine legend says that it began when Kayaomi Narumi no Mikoto, the fifth-generation descendant, built a shrine in the thatched shrine and enshrined his life.<ref name="map" />

=== Outline ===
{{ill|lt=Imperial Court (Japan)|Q30253177|qid=Q30253177|lt=Imperial Court|1=Imperial Court of Japan}}, it was deeply revered, and in national history {{ill|lt=Jowa (Japan)|Q1075605|qid=Q1075605|lt=Jowa|1=Jōwa (Heian period)}} The first record is that he received the lower rank of Junior Fourth Rank (Junior Fourth Rank) in the year 14 ({{ill|lt=847|Q30354|qid=Q30354|1=847}}), and the following year he was promoted to the upper rank of Junior Fourth Rank (Junior Fourth Rank). In the second year of {{ill|lt=Ninju|Q1373077|qid=Q1373077|1=Ninju}} ({{ill|lt=852|Q30383|qid=Q30383|1=852}}), the divine rank was (Rank)|Q240268|Dignity}} Instead of Hon'i, he was given Shihon, and in {{ill|lt=10th century|Q8052|qid=Q8052|1=10th century}}, he was promoted to Ippon.

{{ill|lt=extension (era name)|Q1327274|qid=Q1327274|lt=Extended|1=Enchō}} "{{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|qid=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}}" established in 5 years ({{ill|lt=927 years|Q30470|qid=Q30470|1=927}}) {{j alink|Engishiki Shinmeicho|Q11064932|Shinmeicho}}, {{ill|lt=Bichū|Q1047514|qid=Q1047514|1=Bitchū Province}}{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kayagun|1=UNKNOWN}} has "Kibitsuhiko Shrine <ref>{{ill|lt=Kogakukan University|Q1797584|qid=Q1797584|1=Kogakkan University}} Publishing Department, ``Shikinaisha Investigation Report Vol. 22 Sanyo Road'', listed as ``Meishin University'' and listed in {{ill|lt=Meishin Taisha|Q9610964|qid=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}}. </ref>.In the early {{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|qid=Q193292|1=Heian period}}, Kibitsu Shrine was given {{ill|lt=fūto|Q11458391|qid=Q11458391|1=Fūko}} by the imperial court, and the shrine area expanded by adding donated land, and eventually it was donated to the government and turned into a {{ill|lt=manor (Japan)|Q1887569|qid=Q1887569|lt=manor|1=Shōen}}<ref name="Early modern times">{{Cite journal |Japanese book |author= Hiroshi Shinomiya |title=Aspects of the Separation of Shinto and Buddhism at Kibitsu Shrine in Early Modern Times |journal= Rissho University Graduate School Annual Report |volume=39 |issue= |publisher=Rissho University Graduate School of Letters |date= |pages=33-48 |url=https://rissho.repo.nii.ac.jp/records/10438}}</ref>.

In the early {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|qid=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}}, it was donated to {{ill|lt=Ninnaji|Q1202871|qid=Q1202871|1=Ninna-ji}} and became its fief, but during the {{ill|lt=Nanbokucho period (Japan)|Q264012|qid=Q264012|lt=Nanbokucho period|1=Nanboku-chō period}}, control of Ninnaji Temple was threatened due to conflicts<ref name="Early modern" />. Furthermore, after the {{ill|lt=Onin War|Q385676|qid=Q385676|1=Ōnin War}}, shrine territory was sometimes controlled by the {{ill|lt=Hosokawa clan|Q2736120|qid=Q2736120|1=Hosokawa clan}}, the protector of Bicchu Province, and the Matsuda clan, a local power.<ref name="Early Modern Period" />At the end of the {{ill|lt=Sengoku period (Japan)|Q204023|qid=Q204023|lt=Sengoku period|1=Sengoku period}}, the power of the {{ill|lt=Mori clan|Q914637|qid=Q914637|1=Mōri clan}} came to power, and {{ill|lt=Muneharu Shimizu|Q1046032|qid=Q1046032|1=Shimizu Muneharu}} and others effectively ruled <ref name="Early modern" />. Furthermore, after the unification of the country by {{ill|lt=Toyotomi Hideyoshi|Q187550|qid=Q187550|1=Toyotomi Hideyoshi}}, it became the territory of {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Hideie Ukita|1=UNKNOWN}} and received a donation of shrine territory<ref name="Early Modern Period" />. Furthermore, the ruling relationship with Ninna-ji Temple continued until the early modern period, and it was particularly influenced by rituals.In the {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|qid=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, it first came under the control of the Bicchu magistrate {{ill|lt=Masatsugu Kobori|Q11459515|qid=Q11459515|1=Kobori Masatsugu}} as a territory under the direct control of the {{ill|lt=Tokugawa clan|Q1412769|qid=Q1412769|1=Tokugawa clan}}, and then Miyauchi village, where Kibitsu Shrine is located, became the {{ill|lt=Niwase clan|Q3034514|qid=Q3034514|1=Niwase Domain}} territory.<ref name="Early modern" />. In April of Keicho 6 ({{ill|lt=1601|Q6725|qid=Q6725|1=1601}}), 160 koku were donated by {{ill|lt=Tokugawa Ieyasu|Q171977|qid=Q171977|1=Tokugawa Ieyasu}}, and after deducting the 60 koku of land removed from the precincts, the total was 100 koku, maintaining the stone height of the Ukita clan era<ref name="Early Modern" />. However, since the {{ill|lt=shuinjo|Q11093673|qid=Q11093673|1=shuinjō}} was simply written as ``Bicchu Imperial Palace Shinryo,'' there was a dispute between the shrine family and the shrine monks within the shrine over the destination<ref name="Early Modern Period" />. In the 3rd year of Kanbun ({{ill|lt=1663|Q7577|qid=Q7577|1=1663}}), the ruling of {{ill|lt=Temple and Shrine Bugyo|Q1036279|qid=Q1036279|1=Jisha-bugyō}} allowed shrine and family members to manage the main petition, and the red seals from 5th year of Kanbun ({{ill|lt=1665|Q7582|qid=Q7582|1=1665}}) clearly stated "Kibitsu Daimyo Shrine territory"<ref name="Early modern" />. Even after that, Buddhist facilities such as the {{ill|lt=three-storied pagoda|Q11357687|qid=Q11357687|1=Three-storied pagoda}} remained in the precincts, and monks continued to read sutras in front of the shrine<ref name="Early Modern Period" />.However, in the 14th year of Kyoho ({{ill|lt=1729|Q6952|qid=Q6952|1=1729}}), the shrine and family groups requested the temple magistrate to remove the three-storied pagoda, claiming that it was difficult to repair due to age-related deterioration and was dangerous in the event of wind, rain, or earthquakes, and the Buddhist side requested an injunction. The debate between the two leaders went beyond the pros and cons of removing the three-storied pagoda, to whether the shrine should be a syncretistic shrine of Shintoism and Buddhism, or whether the syncretism of Shintoism and Buddhism should be abolished as the only Shinto shrine.<ref name="Early Modern Period" /> The temple magistrate was the only one who did not directly judge whether it was Shinto or not, but it approved the removal of the three-storied pagoda, denied that the temple had anything to do with the shrine, and determined that the temple had no qualifications to sue. Buddhist buildings such as the three-storied pagoda have disappeared from maps of the precincts from the Kyoho period onward, and it is believed that the separation of Shinto and Buddhism was completed during this period.

After the {{ill|lt=Meiji Restoration|Q8707|qid=Q8707|1=Meiji Restoration}}, in {{ill|lt=Meiji|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}4 ({{ill|lt=1871|Q7746|qid=Q7746|1=1871}}), it was ranked as {{ill|UNKNOWN|qid=Q134917280|lt=Kokuheichusha|1=Kokuhei Chūsha}} in the {{ill|lt=Modern Shrine Ranking System|Q712534|qid=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, and its official name was changed to the current ``Kibitsu Shrine''. In {{ill|lt=1914|Q2083|qid=Q2083|1=1914}} ({{ill|lt=Taisho|Q329174|qid=Q329174|1=Taishō era}}3), it was promoted to {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kanpei Chusha|1=UNKNOWN}}.

=== God level ===
* Records of divine investitures during the Six Kingdoms Historical Period
** {{ill|lt=Jowa (Japan)|Q1075605|qid=Q1075605|lt=Jowa|1=Jōwa (Heian period)}} October 22, 14 years ({{ill|lt=847 years|Q30354|qid=Q30354|1=847}}), from no rank to junior fourth rank ('{{ill|lt=Zoku Nihon Koku|Q1041301|qid=Q1041301|1=Shoku Nihon Kōki}}') - Written as 'Kibitsuhiko no Mikoto'.
** February 21, 1st year of {{ill|lt=Kasho|Q1373157|qid=Q1373157|1=Kashō}} ({{ill|lt=848 years|Q30380|qid=Q30380|1=848}}), Junior Fourth Rank (Shoku Nihon Koki) - Written as ``Kibitsuhiko no Mikoto.''
** On February 20, {{ill|lt=Ninju|Q1373077|qid=Q1373077|1=Ninju}} 2nd year ({{ill|lt=852 years|Q30383|qid=Q30383|1=852}}), Shihon was listed as a government shrine (``{{ill|lt=Nihon Montoku Emperor Annals|Q1052905|qid=Q1052905|1=Nihon Montoku Tennō Jitsuroku}}'') - It is written as ``Kibitsuhiko no Mikoto.''
