=== Precincts ===

=== Main shrine and worship hall ===
[[File:Kibitsu Jinja -01.jpg|thumb|400px|right|{{center|Main shrine and worship hall (national treasure)}}]]The existing main shrine and worship hall were built in the first year of {{ill|lt=Meitoku|Q1145032|qid=Q1145032|1=Meitoku}} of the {{ill|lt=Muromachi period|Q334845|qid=Q334845|1=Muromachi period}} ({{ill|lt=1390|Q6375|qid=Q6375|1=1390}}) under the orders of {{ill|lt=Emperor Gokogon|Q555465|qid=Q555465|1=Emperor Go-Kōgon}}.{{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Muromachi Shogunate|1=UNKNOWN}}The third shogun Yoshimitsu Ashikaga|Q248381}} started construction and moved to {{ill|lt=Oei|Q1193078|qid=Q1193078|1=Ōei}} in 1425.<ref>[http://www.kibitujinja.com/about/honden.html Shrine official website], [http://www.pref.okayama.jp/cgi-bin/bunka/culture/controller/client/resultDetail.cgi?id=4 "Okayama's Cultural Properties"] (Okayama Prefecture website), [https://bunka.nii.ac.jp/heritages/detail/195650 Cultural Heritage Online] etc. indicate that the main shrine was built in 1425 ({{ill|lt=1425|Q6283|qid=Q6283|1=1425}}). ``Weekly Asahi Encyclopedia Japan's National Treasures'' No. 29 (Asahi Shimbun, 1997) states that the main shrine was completed in the 12th year of Oei ({{ill|lt=1405|Q6124|qid=Q6124|1=1405}}), and that the temporary roof was replaced with cypress bark in the 32nd year of Oei, and the shrine was officially transferred to the shrine. </ref>. A gabled and flat worship hall is connected to the front of the main hall, which is built with a gabled gable structure. Hiyoku Irimoya-zukuri is a roof style in which two {{ill|lt=Irimoya-zukuri|Q855025|qid=Q855025|1=East Asian hip-and-gable roof}} roofs are arranged one behind the other, and is also called ``Kibitsu-zukuri.''The size of the main shrine is comparable to the main shrine of {{ill|lt=Izumo Taisha|Q696362|qid=Q696362|1=Izumo-taisha}} and {{ill|lt=Yasaka Shrine|Q692714|qid=Q692714|1=Yasaka Shrine}}, and the influence of Buddhist architecture can be seen everywhere. It is one step higher than the ground, and is built on a stucco-coated earthen platform (Kamebukuro), with a floor plan of 5 ken in the front, 7 ken in the back, and 8 ken in beam space, and the roof is {{ill|lt=cypress bark|Q90373477|qid=Q90373477|1=cypress bark roof}}. Inside, there is a closed inner hall in the center and an inner hall in front of it, surrounded by a lower middle hall, and in front of the middle hall is an even lower vermillion platform (Akenodan), which is surrounded by an even lower outer hall. In this way, it has a unique structure in which the floor height gradually increases from the outside to the inside. The upper half of the wall is surrounded by renji windows, which are rare for shrines. It is said to be the only example of {{ill|lt=Daibutsu-sama|Q3012037|qid=Q3012037|1=Daibutsuyō}} (daibutsuyo) applied to the main hall of a shrine, such as the shape of the inset wood, the bowl, and the rainbow beam.

The worship hall was built at the same time as the main shrine, with three girders (side lines) and one beam (front) with gables; the front is gabled and the back is connected to the main shrine. There are mokoshi floors on the front and sides. The roof is made of cypress bark like the main hall, but the lower floor is made of tile. These main shrines and worship halls are designated as {{ill|lt=National Treasure|Q1139795|qid=Q1139795|1=National Treasure (Japan)}}. - 283</ref><ref name="Main shrine and worship hall"/>.

=== Other shrine buildings ===
* Okamaden
*: {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|qid=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, {{ill|lt=Keicho|Q1328185|qid=Q1328185|1=Keichō}}11 ({{ill|lt=1606|Q6747|qid=Q6747|1=1606}}) Rebuilt by miner Chitane Yasuhara. Single-story, flat-sided roof with real tiles. It is a rectangular shape extending north and south, with a cauldron placed in the north two spaces. A special ritual called "{{ill|lt=Narukama Shinji|Q11675410|qid=Q11675410|1=narukama shinji}}" is held every day except Friday. <ref name="Okamaden"/> has been designated as a national important cultural property.
* Corridor
*: It is said to have been built during {{ill|lt=Sengoku period (Japan)|Q204023|qid=Q204023|lt=Sengoku period|1=Sengoku period}}, between {{ill|lt=Tensho|Q1145058|qid=Q1145058|1=Tenshō (Momoyama period)}} ({{ill|lt=1573|Q6678|qid=Q6678|1=1573}} - {{ill|lt=1591|Q6766|qid=Q6766|1=1591}}). Total length 398 meters. <ref name="Corridor"/> has been designated as a cultural property by Okayama Prefecture.
* Kitazuishinmon
*: Rebuilt in 11 ({{ill|lt=1542|Q6520|qid=Q6520|1=1542}}) during the Muromachi period. Single-layer gabled cypress bark roof. It was built on the approach from the front of the shrine. <ref name="Kitazuishinmon"/> has been designated as a national important cultural property.
* Minamizuishinmon*: Reconstruction in {{ill|lt=Nanbokucho period (Japan)|Q264012|qid=Q264012|lt=Nanbokucho period|1=Nanboku-chō period}}, {{ill|lt=Enbun|Q1145071|qid=Q1145071|1=Enbun}}2 ({{ill|lt=1357|Q6135|qid=Q6135|1=1357}}). The oldest Kibitsu Shrine. Single-layer gable roof with tile roof. It was built in the middle of the corridor to Hongu Shrine. <ref name="Minamizuishinmon"/> is designated as a national important cultural property.
