'''Surroundings'''
[[File:Nakayama Chausuyama Kofun, haisho.jpg|thumb|220px|right|{{center|{{ill|lt=Nakayama Chausuyama Kofun|Q11364273|qid=Q11364273|1=Nakayama Chausuyama Kofun}}}}{{small|The Imperial Household Agency-regulated tomb of Daikibitsuhiko no Mikoto. }}]]* {{ill|lt=Nakayama of Kibi|Q135646238|qid=Q135646238|1=Mount Kibi no Nakayama}}
*: Many ancient tombs and ancient ritual sites remain in Nakayama, Kibi, at the rear of the temple grounds, and it is believed that it was worshiped as {{ill|lt=Shintaiyama|Q1595289|qid=Q1595289|1=Sacred mountains}} since ancient times. At the summit of Mt. Chausu (160 meters) in the center, there is an ancient tomb believed to be the grave of Daikibitsuhiko-no-Mikoto, and at the summit of the highest peak, Mt. Ryuo (northern peak, 175 meters above sea level), there is {{ill|lt=Iwaza|Q11587880|qid=Q11587880|1=Iwakura rock}}, where Kibitsuhiko Shrine is enshrined.
** {{ill|lt=Nakayama Chausuyama Tumulus|Q11364273|qid=Q11364273|1=Nakayama Chausuyama Kofun}} ​​- {{ill|lt=keyhole-shaped tumulus|Q11268718|qid=Q11268718|1=Zenpō-kōen-fun}} with a length of 120 meters. It has been designated by the Imperial Household Agency as the ``Grave of Great Kibitsuhiko.''
** Kibi Archaeological Museum
* {{ill|lt=Kibitsuhiko Shrine|Q500763|qid=Q500763|1=Kibitsuhiko Shrine}} - Bizen Province Ichinomiya.
* Remains of the residence of the Kayo clan - Believed to be the site of the residence of the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Kayō clan|1=UNKNOWN}} who served as a priest at Kibitsu Shrine. Okayama Prefecture's designated historic site <ref>[http://www.pref.okayama.jp/cgi-bin/bunka/culture/controller/client/resultDetail.cgi?id=325 Dengayoujikan Ruins] {{ja icon}} (Okayama Prefecture homepage "Okayama Cultural Properties"). </ref>.
* {{ill|lt=Myoan Eishi|Q366128|qid=Q366128|lt=Eishi|1=Eisai}}Monument of the birthplace of the monk
* {{ill|lt=Zoriyama Kofun|Q11639745|qid=Q11639745|1=Tsukuriyama Kofun (Okayama)}} - The 4th largest keyhole-shaped tumulus in Japan.

